{
  "praise_line": "Two things really worked.",
  "wins": [
    {
      "behaviour": "asked a student to explain and waited, which drew the class into reasoning",
      "evidence": "when a student gave an answer, you asked why, and waited, and she explained her reasoning about the shaded parts. That pushed the whole class to think."
    },
    {
      "behaviour": "gave a clear, concrete definition that students repeated",
      "evidence": "your definition of the numerator was very clear. You pointed at the shaded parts on the board and said, \"The top number counts them,\" and the students repeated it correctly."
    }
  ],
  "concern": null,
  "try": {
    "move": "Before you finish the debrief, ask the teacher to say or show exactly how they will run the think–pair–share once, and wait silently while they rehearse it.",
    "evidence": "So your commitment is to use \"think, pair, share\" at least twice in your next lesson. I will come back in 2 weeks to see how it goes.",
    "instead": "After securing the commitment, prompt the teacher to role‑play the words/actions and hold silence until they finish the rehearsal."
  },
  "reflection_question": "When you answered the teacher's doubt with \"It was,\" what effect did that have on the teacher's opportunity to explain and learn?",
  "value": "heshima",
  "focus": "rehearsed_the_step",
  "focus_why": "Teacher agreed to try think–pair–share but did not practice it; rehearsal makes the commitment concrete before you leave.",
